  "description": "A 300 kg probe in an 18 000 km x 0.2 orbit around Mars. A 40 deg, 512 px camera sees the planet's disk; its place and size give a position, and Basilisk's relativeODuKF filter estimates the orbit from those. The filter starts 68 km off. 2 days.\n\nWhat to look at:\n- probe-1.optical_navigation.position_error: from 68 km to under 2 km by day 1 (1.6 km median after it); up to 12 km just after each night-side stretch, inside the filter's own 3-sigma.\n- probe-1.optical_navigation.measurement_error: one image alone is good to 56 km (median); the filter does better.\n- probe-1.optical_navigation.measurement_state and .phase_angle: night-side images (phase over 90 deg) are not used; 62 % are.\n- probe-1.optical_navigation.measurement: the disk is 110 to 152 px in radius over the orbit.\n\nTry changing:\n- Disk centre and radius noise (probe-1's Optical navigation) to 0: every image gives the true position.\n- Largest phase angle to 180 deg: every image is used, the night side too.\n\nNote:\n- The images are computed from the true geometry with 0.5 px noise, not rendered: a crescent's centre is not offset as a real one would be. Mars is its system barycentre here.",
